Play that clip again

00:06:14.735 --> 00:06:17.665
In the movie, there's another crucial distinction:

00:06:17.665 --> 00:06:20.805
The apartment jumper sues the government, not the

00:06:20.805 --> 00:06:23.855
Super as an individual. This last twist is

00:06:23.855 --> 00:06:26.855
where things get really interesting. In the movie, we're told that

00:06:26.855 --> 00:06:29.825
the Supers received amnesty for their past actions

00:06:29.825 --> 00:06:32.755
as part of their agreement to go into hiding.

00:06:32.755 --> 00:06:36.075
"The government quietly initiated the Superhero Relocation Program.

00:06:36.075 --> 00:06:39.035
"The Supers would be granted amnesty from responsibility for past actions

00:06:39.035 --> 00:06:42.195
"in exchange for the promise to never again

00:06:42.295 --> 00:06:45.265
"resume hero work." But in reality, Supers

00:06:45.265 --> 00:06:48.375
aren't liable in the first place. See, the Supers Program

00:06:48.375 --> 00:06:51.215
is a government program and falls under a

00:06:51.215 --> 00:06:54.205
doctrine in the United States known as  Qualified Immunity.

00:06:54.205 --> 00:06:57.205
which protects states and private officials from getting sued

00:06:57.205 --> 00:07:00.315
except in specific cases like discrimination, or when they

00:07:00.315 --> 00:07:03.385
use unnecessary force. It's clear from the moments we see

00:07:03.385 --> 00:07:06.415
the Supers working with the police that they are a kind of special branch

00:07:06.415 --> 00:07:09.275
of law enforcement, kind of like the FBI. And they're

00:07:09.275 --> 00:07:12.025
acting under similar principals as the police, prioritizing

00:07:12.025 --> 00:07:14.995
civilian safety, taking every available action to prevent casualties,

00:07:14.995 --> 00:07:17.625
and so on. So when the jumper sues the Supers,

00:07:17.625 --> 00:07:20.795
he's basically suing a government agency,

00:07:20.795 --> 00:07:23.795
or a branch of law enforcement. Which then begs the question, can you even

00:07:23.795 --> 00:07:26.735
do that? In our modern society, we've pretty much taken for granted

00:07:26.735 --> 00:07:29.695
that you can sue anyone. But for most of U.S. history,

00:07:29.695 --> 00:07:32.795
you couldn't sue the government at all. Seems... fishy,

00:07:32.795 --> 00:07:35.705
right? Especially given that the government gets involved in some kinda

00:07:35.705 --> 00:07:38.745
questionable situations from time to time, everything from the bizzaro

00:07:38.745 --> 00:07:41.595
conspiracies of MK Ultra to the very up-to-the-moment

00:07:41.595 --> 00:07:44.420
protests around police discrimination. The law

00:07:44.505 --> 00:07:46.585
that protects governments from suits is called

00:07:46.585 --> 00:07:49.205
Sovereign immunity, and quite frankly, sounds like something you'd hear

00:07:49.205 --> 00:07:52.255
coming from King George during the musical, 'Hamilton'. It basically

00:07:52.255 --> 00:07:55.475
says that a sovereign state cannot commit a legal wrong

00:07:55.475 --> 00:07:58.465
and has immunity both for civil lawsuits and criminal

00:07:58.465 --> 00:08:01.545
prosecution. If that seems like the government's a little OP for you,

00:08:01.545 --> 00:08:03.755
well, most of the U.S. agreed, and decided

00:08:03.755 --> 00:08:06.695
back in 1946 to pass the Federal

00:08:06.695 --> 00:08:09.645
Tort Claims Act, which made it possible to sue the government for

00:08:09.645 --> 00:08:12.335
damages, negligence, and other governmental

00:08:12.335 --> 00:08:15.115
wrongdoings. That doesn't mean that actually pulling off a lawsuit against

00:08:15.115 --> 00:08:18.175
the government isn't INCREDIBLY difficult-- (no pun

00:08:18.175 --> 00:08:21.225
intended--maybe pun kind of intended)-- even in situations we see

00:08:21.225 --> 00:08:24.035
every day, like a car that gets damaged by police in a

00:08:24.035 --> 00:08:27.065
high-speed chase, or a window that gets broken in a police shoot-out,

00:08:27.065 --> 00:08:29.835
taking the government to task-- or, more specifically,

00:08:29.835 --> 00:08:32.205
taking the government to court because of the collateral

00:08:32.205 --> 00:08:35.055
damage-- is still super difficult and costly as a

00:08:35.055 --> 00:08:36.705
regular citizen or small business.

00:08:38.205 --> 00:08:41.305
In reality, suing a super for trying to save someone's life,

00:08:41.305 --> 00:08:44.405
even if they destroyed private property along the way, it would be

00:08:44.405 --> 00:08:47.115
almost impossible. So, there you go.

00:08:47.115 --> 00:08:50.335
Based on the laws that exist, it seems like this is a pretty open-and-shut case.

00:08:50.335 --> 00:08:53.515
You can't be sued for trying to help save someone, even in the case of suicide.

00:08:53.515 --> 00:08:56.585
Government officials all have qualified immunity, and

00:08:56.585 --> 00:08:58.845
it would be pretty impossible to show that supers are

00:08:58.845 --> 00:09:01.675
violating the few laws that they can be sued over. In short,

00:09:01.675 --> 00:09:04.265
you can't sue supers into hiding-- if the case

